README.OSK -- the rogue 5.3 clone on OS-9/68000ORIG/ is Tim Stoehr's rogue 5.3 clone as comp.sources.games posted it,volume 1 issues 11 to 15 (1987), and ORIG/PATCH1, PATCH2 and PATCH3 are itsthree patches as released: v01i032, v01i052 and v12i097 (1991, which addedMike Zraly's VMS port).This tree is the posting with all three patches applied.  Patches01 and 03apply cleanly; Patches02's two machdep.c hunks do not, because Patch1 andPatch3 each ship a whole new machdep.c, and Patch3's already carries them.The author put every system dependence in machdep.c, in UNIX, UNIX_BSD4_2,UNIX_SYSV and VMS arms, and asked for ports to add their own.  This is anOSK arm.  It builds with -DOSK -DCURSES (the game's own curses, which readsTERM and TERMCAP), without the UNIX defines.  machdep.c   an #ifdef OSK arm in each md_ function that could not work:              md_slurp drains typeahead with _gs_rdy(); md_gln takes USER;              md_gdtcf names /dd/SYS/termcap; md_cbreak_no_echo_nonl turns              echo off and back on.  md_control_keybord clears ^C, ^E, ESC              and ^W while the game runs and puts them back -- OS-9's              driver turns ^C and ^E into signals, reads ESC, the game's              cancel key, as end of file, and pauses on ^W.  Its sense              follows the code, not the comment above it: start_window()              calls it with 0.  Each of those two routines restores only              the settings it changed; restoring a whole saved copy put              echo back off after the game.  md_get_file_id, md_link_count              and md_gfmt are the stubs the author's comments allow (0, 1,              and a time in the past), for RBF has no inodes or hard links.              A new md_getchar() reads one key with read(): OS-9's stdio              reads a terminal a line at a time.  rogue.h     under OSK, getchar() is md_getchar(), with <stdio.h>'s macro              undefined first; and the old `char *sprintf();' is left out,              since OS-9's <stdio.h> declares it int.  score.c     the score file is /dd/GAMES/ROGUE/rogue.scores.MEASURED 2026-09-15 under os9exec: it moves, opens the inventory, ESCcancels a prompt, ^C does no harm, Q and y quit to the Top Ten; theterminal's interrupt, abort, end-of-file characters and echo are as theywere afterwards; and two players' scores both stay in the file.